WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.675
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.975 --> 00:00:09.338
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.638 --> 00:00:15.472
Unit three: Cleft Sentences. Today you will focus attention on one piece of information.

4
00:00:15.772 --> 00:00:18.238
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.502
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:24.607
It was... that.

7
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:28.603
It was... that means a cleft frame that focuses a word.

8
00:00:29.217 --> 00:00:30.671
It was Sarah that won.

9
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:33.576
Your turn. Say: It was... that.

10
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:37.469
It was... that.

11
00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:39.102
What... is.

12
00:00:40.117 --> 00:00:43.219
What... is means a cleft frame that focuses an idea.

13
00:00:43.817 --> 00:00:45.387
What I need is rest.

14
00:00:45.983 --> 00:00:48.485
Your turn. Say: What... is.

15
00:00:51.333 --> 00:00:52.427
What... is.

16
00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:54.312
Cleft structure.

17
00:00:55.317 --> 00:00:58.326
Cleft structure means a sentence split to add focus.

18
00:00:58.917 --> 00:01:00.336
A cleft structure.

19
00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.614
Your turn. Say: Cleft structure.

20
00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:08.304
Cleft structure.

21
00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:10.016
Focus.

22
00:01:11.017 --> 00:01:13.632
Focus means the important part of a message.

23
00:01:14.217 --> 00:01:15.717
The focus is the winner.

24
00:01:16.317 --> 00:01:18.876
Your turn. Say: Focus.

25
00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:22.630
Focus.

26
00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:24.373
Highlight.

27
00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:27.731
Highlight means to make something stand out.

28
00:01:28.317 --> 00:01:29.875
Highlight the key idea.

29
00:01:30.483 --> 00:01:32.915
Your turn. Say: Highlight.

30
00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:36.710
Highlight.

31
00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.556
Emphasis.

32
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:42.571
Emphasis means special importance given to something.

33
00:01:43.183 --> 00:01:44.951
Put emphasis on the result.

34
00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.109
Your turn. Say: Emphasis.

35
00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:52.188
Emphasis.

36
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.706
Information structure.

37
00:01:55.717 --> 00:01:59.469
Information structure means how new and old facts are ordered.

38
00:02:00.083 --> 00:02:02.454
Information structure guides readers.

39
00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:06.411
Your turn. Say: Information structure.

40
00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:10.988
Information structure.

41
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:12.832
Rhetorical.

42
00:02:13.817 --> 00:02:15.956
Rhetorical means used to persuade.

43
00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:18.039
A rhetorical effect.

44
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:21.268
Your turn. Say: Rhetorical.

45
00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:25.251
Rhetorical.

46
00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:29.822
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:36.157
Visal, Sarah won the competition. How can I stress that it was Sarah, not someone else?

48
00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:45.928
Use a cleft: It was Sarah who won the competition. The sentence is split into two clauses so that Sarah gets the focus.

49
00:02:47.017 --> 00:02:49.063
And to stress the action or the thing?

50
00:02:50.150 --> 00:03:01.327
Use a wh-cleft: What she wanted was the trophy. What Sarah did was train every morning. The focused idea comes last, after the verb be.

51
00:03:02.417 --> 00:03:04.265
Why is this useful in writing?

52
00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:16.222
It controls information structure. Familiar information comes first and the new, important point comes at the end, where readers notice it most.

53
00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:18.774
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:19.617 --> 00:03:27.015
Grammar focus: Cleft: It was plus focus plus that and who  ; What plus clause plus is plus focus.

55
00:03:27.315 --> 00:03:28.560
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.517
It was Sarah who won the competition.

57
00:03:32.417 --> 00:03:34.289
What I need is a long rest.

58
00:03:35.183 --> 00:03:37.403
It was last June that we first met.

59
00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:44.479
It-cleft: It was X that / who... Wh-cleft: What ... is X. Both place the key information in focus.

60
00:03:45.217 --> 00:03:48.693
Be careful! Don't say: It was Tom what called me.

61
00:03:50.633 --> 00:03:52.958
Say: It was Tom who called me.

62
00:03:53.258 --> 00:03:54.735
Use who for a person.

63
00:03:55.533 --> 00:03:58.035
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:58.833 --> 00:04:00.229
Putting the Focus.

65
00:04:00.529 --> 00:04:02.529
It was my brother who broke the window.

66
00:04:03.533 --> 00:04:05.277
What I need is a long rest.

67
00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:08.382
All I want is some peace and quiet.

68
00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:11.413
It was last June that we first met.

69
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.048
Each sentence stresses the main idea by splitting the message in two.

70
00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:21.135
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:21.983 --> 00:04:23.635
It blank Tom who called me.

72
00:04:23.935 --> 00:04:27.158
A, was. B, did. C, has.

73
00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.618
The answer is A: was.

74
00:04:33.783 --> 00:04:35.353
blank I need is a rest.

75
00:04:35.653 --> 00:04:38.761
A, That. B, What. C, Which.

76
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.882
The answer is B: What.

77
00:04:45.017 --> 00:04:47.190
Which sentence is a cleft sentence?

78
00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:52.142
A, Sarah won.. B, It was Sarah who won.. C, Sarah has won..

79
00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.036
The answer is B: It was Sarah who won..

80
00:04:59.183 --> 00:05:01.009
Why use a cleft sentence?

81
00:05:01.309 --> 00:05:05.752
A, to add focus. B, to ask a question. C, to shorten.

82
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.950
The answer is A: to add focus.

83
00:05:13.083 --> 00:05:15.629
According to the reading, who broke the window?

84
00:05:15.929 --> 00:05:19.779
A, my sister. B, my brother. C, my friend.

85
00:05:23.333 --> 00:05:25.344
The answer is B: my brother.

86
00:05:26.533 --> 00:05:30.045
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:30.883 --> 00:05:34.708
Rewrite this as a cleft sentence: 'Sarah won the competition.'

88
00:05:43.517 --> 00:05:45.087
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:45.387 --> 00:05:55.878
It was Sarah who won the competition. Another option is: What Sarah did was win the competition. The cleft focuses the reader's attention on the winner.

90
00:05:56.178 --> 00:05:58.166
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:07.705
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and cleft: it was plus focus plus that and who  ; what plus clause plus is plus focus.

92
00:06:08.005 --> 00:06:13.107
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

93
00:06:13.407 --> 00:06:15.920
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

94
00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:19.278
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

